In general position, a set of $n$ points in $\mathbb{R}^{n-1}$ is linearly separable. Therefore, if $n-1$ or less points from $\mathcal{M}=\{\boldsymbol{x}_1, \ldots , \boldsymbol{x}_{M-1}\}$ are not separated from $\boldsymbol{x}$ by the hyperplane $L$ (Fig.~\ref{Fig:Balls}) then they can be separated by an additional hyperplane orthogonal to $L$. This means that  $\boldsymbol{x}$  can be separated from the whole $\mathcal{M}$ by a
conjunction of two linear inequalities,
$(\bullet , \boldsymbol{x}/\|\boldsymbol{x}\|) >r \, \& \, (\bullet , \boldsymbol{y}) > q$, for some $0<r<0$, $q>0$, and $\boldsymbol{y}$, $(\boldsymbol{y},\boldsymbol{x})=0$. This system can be considered as a cascade of two independent neurons \citep{GorbanRomBurtTyu2016}.  The probability of such a {\em two-neuron separability} is higher than of linear separability. (Compare inequality (\ref{Eq:ball1n}) in the following theorem to (\ref{Eq:ball1}).)
